      ]
    },
    "description": "The Checkout API authenticates a customer and delegates card-funded order placement to the Funds API, which calls the Ledger Service and Payment Gateway. Security Operations requires a uniform record for every attempt, including requests rejected by authorization, calls that fail downstream, and successful transfers. Each event must identify the actor, action, target, correlation ID, attempt, and terminal result. Because the Ledger Service can roll back its database transaction, its audit record must not disappear with that rollback. The Funds API also needs a policy applied at one invocation boundary so engineers do not hand-code different schemas in Checkout, Ledger, and Payment Gateway. Events must be sent outside the business transaction to a separate integrity-protected destination, and the design must explicitly acknowledge that direct calls bypassing the Funds API are outside its coverage.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose where to enforce one consistent policy that captures attributable, outcome-aware security events around delegated payment operations without relying on each service to emit its own records.",
    "notes": "One invocation-boundary policy captures attributable attempt-and-outcome events outside the business transaction; secure-logger covers the tamper-evidence axis as the near miss."
  },
